# Speed Cards starter deck: Spanish (Mexico). seed-version: 1

# ===== Topic 1: Travel survival =====
@deck Supervivencia / Viaje
@type Spanish Vocab
@tags travel
@languages es-MX en-US

id: seed-es-travel-001
Spanish: el baño
English: bathroom, restroom
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Disculpe, ¿dónde está el {{c1::baño}}?
Example (EN): Excuse me, where is the bathroom?
Notes: Also "el sanitario". In Spain often "el servicio" or "el aseo". Doors may say "Damas / Caballeros".

id: seed-es-travel-002
Spanish: hola
English: hello, hi
Gender / POS: interjection
Example (ES): {{c1::Hola}}, ¿cómo estás?
Example (EN): Hi, how are you?
Notes: The h is silent.

id: seed-es-travel-003
Spanish: buenos días
English: good morning
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): {{c1::Buenos días}}, señor. ¿Cómo está?
Example (EN): Good morning, sir. How are you?
Notes: Used until about noon, then "buenas tardes". "Buen día" is also heard in Mexico.

id: seed-es-travel-004
Spanish: buenas noches
English: good evening, good night
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): {{c1::Buenas noches}}, ¿tienen una mesa para dos?
Example (EN): Good evening, do you have a table for two?
Notes: Used both as a greeting after dark and when saying goodbye.

id: seed-es-travel-005
Spanish: por favor
English: please
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): Un café, {{c1::por favor}}.
Example (EN): A coffee, please.

id: seed-es-travel-006
Spanish: gracias
English: thank you, thanks
Gender / POS: interjection
Example (ES): Muchas {{c1::gracias}} por su ayuda.
Example (EN): Thank you very much for your help.
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-007]]

id: seed-es-travel-007
Spanish: de nada
English: you're welcome
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): —Gracias. —{{c1::De nada}}.
Example (EN): "Thanks." "You're welcome."
Notes: Also "por nada". Very common in Mexico: "a sus órdenes" or "para servirle".

id: seed-es-travel-008
Spanish: disculpe
English: excuse me, sorry (formal)
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): {{c1::Disculpe}}, ¿habla inglés?
Example (EN): Excuse me, do you speak English?
Notes: Formal (usted). Informal: "disculpa". "Perdón" is also common.

id: seed-es-travel-009
Spanish: con permiso
English: excuse me (to get past or to leave)
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): {{c1::Con permiso}}, aquí me bajo.
Example (EN): Excuse me, I'm getting off here.
Notes: Said when squeezing past people or leaving a table. Typical Mexican reply: "Propio" or "Adelante".

id: seed-es-travel-010
Spanish: mucho gusto
English: nice to meet you
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): {{c1::Mucho gusto}}, me llamo Ana.
Example (EN): Nice to meet you, my name is Ana.
Notes: Reply: "Igualmente" or "El gusto es mío".

id: seed-es-travel-011
Spanish: ¿Cuánto cuesta?
English: How much does it cost?
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): Disculpe, ¿{{c1::cuánto cuesta}} esta playera?
Example (EN): Excuse me, how much is this T-shirt?
Notes: Plural: "¿Cuánto cuestan?". "¿Cuánto es?" for the total. "La playera" = T-shirt in Mexico (Spain: "la camiseta").

id: seed-es-travel-012
Spanish: la cuenta
English: check, bill
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): ¿Nos trae la {{c1::cuenta}}, por favor?
Example (EN): Could you bring us the check, please?
Notes: Waiters usually won't bring it until you ask.
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-013]]

id: seed-es-travel-013
Spanish: la propina
English: tip, gratuity
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): En México se deja de 10 a 15 % de {{c1::propina}}.
Example (EN): In Mexico you leave a 10 to 15% tip.
Notes: Usually not included in the bill. "¿Incluye el servicio?" = Is service included?

id: seed-es-travel-014
Spanish: el efectivo
English: cash
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¿Puedo pagar en {{c1::efectivo}}?
Example (EN): Can I pay in cash?
Notes: Many small shops and street stalls only take cash.
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-016]]

id: seed-es-travel-015
Spanish: la tarjeta
English: card (credit/debit)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): ¿Aceptan {{c1::tarjeta}}?
Example (EN): Do you take cards?
Notes: "Tarjeta de crédito / de débito".
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-016]]

id: seed-es-travel-016
Spanish: pagar
English: to pay
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): ¿Dónde {{c1::pago}}?
Example (EN): Where do I pay?
Notes: Preterite: yo pagué (spelling change g → gu).

id: seed-es-travel-017
Spanish: el cambio
English: change (money), exchange
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Quédese con el {{c1::cambio}}.
Example (EN): Keep the change.
Notes: "La casa de cambio" = currency exchange office. Mexican slang for small change: "la feria".

id: seed-es-travel-018
Spanish: cien
English: one hundred
Gender / POS: number
Example (ES): Son {{c1::cien}} pesos, por favor.
Example (EN): That's one hundred pesos, please.
Notes: "Cien" alone or before a noun; "ciento" in compounds: ciento veinte (120). 500 = quinientos, 1,000 = mil.

id: seed-es-travel-019
Spanish: barato
English: cheap, inexpensive
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): Este mercado es muy {{c1::barato}}.
Example (EN): This market is very cheap.
links: antonym [[seed-es-travel-020]]

id: seed-es-travel-020
Spanish: caro
English: expensive
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): La comida en el aeropuerto es muy {{c1::cara}}.
Example (EN): The food at the airport is very expensive.
Notes: Agrees in gender and number: caro, cara, caros, caras.

id: seed-es-travel-021
Spanish: ¿Dónde está…?
English: Where is…?
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): ¿{{c1::Dónde está}} la estación del metro?
Example (EN): Where is the subway station?
Notes: Plural: "¿Dónde están…?". "¿Dónde queda…?" is also very common in Mexico.

id: seed-es-travel-022
Spanish: a la derecha
English: to the right, on the right
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): El hotel está {{c1::a la derecha}}.
Example (EN): The hotel is on the right.
links: antonym [[seed-es-travel-023]]

id: seed-es-travel-023
Spanish: a la izquierda
English: to the left, on the left
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): Dé vuelta {{c1::a la izquierda}} en el semáforo.
Example (EN): Turn left at the traffic light.
Notes: Mexico: "dar vuelta" or "voltear"; Spain: "girar".

id: seed-es-travel-024
Spanish: derecho
English: straight ahead
Gender / POS: adv.
Example (ES): Siga todo {{c1::derecho}} dos cuadras.
Example (EN): Go straight ahead for two blocks.
Notes: Spain prefers "recto". Don't confuse with "a la derecha" (to the right).
links: same root [[seed-es-travel-022]]

id: seed-es-travel-025
Spanish: la cuadra
English: block (city block)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La farmacia está a tres {{c1::cuadras}}.
Example (EN): The pharmacy is three blocks away.
Notes: Spain: "la manzana".
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-026]]

id: seed-es-travel-026
Spanish: la esquina
English: corner (street)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Nos vemos en la {{c1::esquina}}.
Example (EN): See you at the corner.
Notes: An inside corner of a room is "el rincón".

id: seed-es-travel-027
Spanish: el camión
English: bus
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Tomamos el {{c1::camión}} al centro.
Example (EN): We take the bus downtown.
Notes: Everyday Mexican word for a city bus (also means truck). Spain: "el autobús". Plural: los camiones.
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-030]]

id: seed-es-travel-028
Spanish: el metro
English: subway, metro
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::metro}} de la Ciudad de México es muy barato.
Example (EN): The Mexico City subway is very cheap.

id: seed-es-travel-029
Spanish: el boleto
English: ticket
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Necesito un {{c1::boleto}} para Oaxaca.
Example (EN): I need a ticket to Oaxaca.
Notes: Spain: "el billete" (which in Mexico means a banknote).

id: seed-es-travel-030
Spanish: la parada
English: stop (bus stop)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): ¿En qué {{c1::parada}} me bajo?
Example (EN): Which stop do I get off at?
Notes: "Bajarse" = to get off; "subirse" = to get on.

id: seed-es-travel-031
Spanish: el aeropuerto
English: airport
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¿Cuánto cuesta un taxi al {{c1::aeropuerto}}?
Example (EN): How much is a taxi to the airport?
Notes: "al" = a + el.

id: seed-es-travel-032
Spanish: la maleta
English: suitcase
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Perdí mi {{c1::maleta}}.
Example (EN): I lost my suitcase.
Notes: "El equipaje" = luggage. "La valija" is used in some other countries.
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-031]]

id: seed-es-travel-033
Spanish: el carro
English: car
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Rentamos un {{c1::carro}} para ir a la playa.
Example (EN): We rented a car to go to the beach.
Notes: Mexico: "carro" or "coche"; Spain: "coche". "Rentar" is Mexican (Spain: "alquilar").

id: seed-es-travel-034
Spanish: ¿Me da…?
English: Can I have…?, Could you give me…?
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): ¿{{c1::Me da}} dos tacos al pastor, por favor?
Example (EN): Can I have two al pastor tacos, please?
Notes: Polite standard way to order or buy (usted). Informal: "¿Me das…?".
links: synonym [[seed-es-travel-035]]

id: seed-es-travel-035
Spanish: ¿Me regalas…?
English: Could I have…? (lit. "will you gift me…?")
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): ¿{{c1::Me regalas}} un vaso de agua?
Example (EN): Could I have a glass of water?
Notes: Friendly, very common way to ask for something in Mexico; you still pay. Formal: "¿Me regala…?".

id: seed-es-travel-036
Spanish: el agua
English: water
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Una botella de {{c1::agua}}, por favor.
Example (EN): A bottle of water, please.
Notes: Feminine but takes "el" (stressed a): el agua fría, las aguas. Tap water: "agua de la llave" (Spain: "del grifo"); drink "agua purificada". "¡Aguas!" = Watch out! (Mexican slang).

id: seed-es-travel-037
Spanish: el menú
English: menu
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¿Me puede traer el {{c1::menú}}?
Example (EN): Can you bring me the menu?
Notes: "La carta" is also used. A cheap set lunch is "la comida corrida" or "el menú del día".

id: seed-es-travel-038
Spanish: picante
English: spicy, hot
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): ¿Esta salsa es muy {{c1::picante}}?
Example (EN): Is this salsa very spicy?
Notes: Common question: "¿Pica?" (Is it spicy?). Ask for "sin chile" if needed.

id: seed-es-travel-039
Spanish: el desayuno
English: breakfast
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¿El {{c1::desayuno}} está incluido?
Example (EN): Is breakfast included?
Notes: Verb: "desayunar". Dinner: "la cena".

id: seed-es-travel-040
Spanish: la comida
English: food, lunch (main midday meal)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::comida}} mexicana es deliciosa.
Example (EN): Mexican food is delicious.
Notes: In Mexico also the main afternoon meal (about 2–4 p.m.); in Spain that meal is often "el almuerzo".

id: seed-es-travel-041
Spanish: el hotel
English: hotel
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Nuestro {{c1::hotel}} está cerca del centro.
Example (EN): Our hotel is near downtown.
Notes: The h is silent.

id: seed-es-travel-042
Spanish: la habitación
English: room (hotel room)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Quisiera una {{c1::habitación}} doble.
Example (EN): I'd like a double room.
Notes: "El cuarto" is very common in Mexico.
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-041]]

id: seed-es-travel-043
Spanish: la reservación
English: reservation, booking
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Tengo una {{c1::reservación}} a nombre de Smith.
Example (EN): I have a reservation under the name Smith.
Notes: Mexico: "reservación"; Spain: "reserva".
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-042]]

id: seed-es-travel-044
Spanish: ¡Ayuda!
English: Help!
Gender / POS: interjection
Example (ES): ¡{{c1::Ayuda}}! ¡Llamen a una ambulancia!
Example (EN): Help! Call an ambulance!
Notes: Also "¡Auxilio!". Emergency number in Mexico: 911.

id: seed-es-travel-045
Spanish: el hospital
English: hospital
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¿Dónde está el {{c1::hospital}} más cercano?
Example (EN): Where is the nearest hospital?
Notes: The h is silent.

id: seed-es-travel-046
Spanish: la farmacia
English: pharmacy, drugstore
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Hay una {{c1::farmacia}} en la esquina.
Example (EN): There's a pharmacy on the corner.

id: seed-es-travel-047
Spanish: la policía
English: police
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Hay que llamar a la {{c1::policía}}.
Example (EN): We need to call the police.
Notes: "La policía" = the police force; "el/la policía" = a police officer.

id: seed-es-travel-048
Spanish: enfermo
English: sick, ill
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): Mi amigo está {{c1::enfermo}}.
Example (EN): My friend is sick.
Notes: Use with "estar": estoy enfermo / enferma. "Me siento mal" = I feel sick.
links: collocation [[seed-es-travel-045]]

id: seed-es-travel-049
Spanish: ¿Qué hora es?
English: What time is it?
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): Disculpe, ¿{{c1::qué hora es}}?
Example (EN): Excuse me, what time is it?
Notes: Answers: "Es la una", "Son las tres".

id: seed-es-travel-050
Spanish: ahorita
English: right now, in a little while
Gender / POS: adv.
Example (ES): {{c1::Ahorita}} vengo.
Example (EN): I'll be right back.
Notes: Very Mexican and flexible: can mean "right now" or "later, eventually"; context and tone decide.

# ===== Topic 2: Archery =====
@deck Tiro con arco
@type Spanish Vocab
@tags archery
@languages es-MX en-US

id: seed-es-archery-001
Spanish: el tiro con arco
English: archery
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Practico {{c1::tiro con arco}} los sábados.
Example (EN): I practice archery on Saturdays.
Notes: "La arquería" is also heard in Mexico.

id: seed-es-archery-002
Spanish: el arquero
English: archer (male)
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::arquero}} apunta al centro.
Example (EN): The archer aims at the center.
Notes: In soccer "arquero" can mean goalkeeper (Mexico usually says "portero").
links: gender pair [[seed-es-archery-003]]; same root [[seed-es-archery-004]]

id: seed-es-archery-003
Spanish: la arquera
English: archer (female)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::arquera}} ganó la medalla de oro.
Example (EN): The archer won the gold medal.

id: seed-es-archery-004
Spanish: el arco
English: bow
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Mi {{c1::arco}} es de 30 libras.
Example (EN): My bow is 30 pounds.
Notes: Also means "arch".

id: seed-es-archery-005
Spanish: la flecha
English: arrow
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Saqué la {{c1::flecha}} de la diana.
Example (EN): I pulled the arrow out of the target.
links: collocation [[seed-es-archery-006]]

id: seed-es-archery-006
Spanish: el carcaj
English: quiver
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Guarda las flechas en el {{c1::carcaj}}.
Example (EN): Keep the arrows in the quiver.
Notes: Plural: los carcajes. Also "la aljaba".

id: seed-es-archery-007
Spanish: la cuerda
English: bowstring, string, rope
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::cuerda}} del arco está muy gastada.
Example (EN): The bowstring is very worn.

id: seed-es-archery-008
Spanish: el emplumado
English: fletching
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::emplumado}} estabiliza la flecha en el aire.
Example (EN): The fletching stabilizes the arrow in flight.
Notes: Individual vanes are "las plumas" (feathers) or "las aletas" (plastic vanes). Verb: "emplumar".
links: collocation [[seed-es-archery-005]]

id: seed-es-archery-009
Spanish: la punta
English: point, arrowhead, tip
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::punta}} de la flecha se dobló.
Example (EN): The arrow point bent.

id: seed-es-archery-010
Spanish: el culatín
English: nock
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Coloca el {{c1::culatín}} en la cuerda.
Example (EN): Put the nock on the string.
Notes: Also just "el nock" in archery shops. Plural: los culatines.
links: collocation [[seed-es-archery-007]]

id: seed-es-archery-011
Spanish: la mira
English: sight
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Ajusta la {{c1::mira}} un poco hacia arriba.
Example (EN): Move the sight up a little.
links: collocation [[seed-es-archery-033]]

id: seed-es-archery-012
Spanish: el estabilizador
English: stabilizer
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::estabilizador}} reduce la vibración del arco.
Example (EN): The stabilizer reduces the bow's vibration.

id: seed-es-archery-013
Spanish: la dactilera
English: finger tab
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Uso una {{c1::dactilera}} de cuero.
Example (EN): I use a leather finger tab.
Notes: Also "el protector de dedos".

id: seed-es-archery-014
Spanish: el brazal
English: arm guard
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Ponte el {{c1::brazal}} para que la cuerda no te pegue.
Example (EN): Put on your arm guard so the string doesn't hit you.
Notes: Also "el protector de antebrazo".
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-041]]

id: seed-es-archery-015
Spanish: la diana
English: target face, target
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::diana}} tiene diez anillos.
Example (EN): The target face has ten rings.
Notes: In Mexico "el blanco" is also very common.
links: synonym [[seed-es-archery-016]]

id: seed-es-archery-016
Spanish: el blanco
English: target, bullseye
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Le di al {{c1::blanco}}.
Example (EN): I hit the target.
Notes: Also the adjective "white". "Dar en el blanco" = to hit the mark.

id: seed-es-archery-017
Spanish: la paja
English: straw (target butt)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Las flechas se quedan clavadas en la {{c1::paja}}.
Example (EN): The arrows stick in the straw.
Notes: The target butt is also called "el parapeto" or "la paca"; many ranges use foam ("espuma").
links: collocation [[seed-es-archery-015]]

id: seed-es-archery-018
Spanish: la distancia
English: distance
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Hoy tiramos a una {{c1::distancia}} de 18 metros.
Example (EN): Today we're shooting at a distance of 18 meters.

id: seed-es-archery-019
Spanish: la línea de tiro
English: shooting line
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Todos detrás de la {{c1::línea de tiro}}.
Example (EN): Everyone behind the shooting line.
Notes: "Tirar" = to shoot (in archery).
links: same root [[seed-es-archery-001]]

id: seed-es-archery-020
Spanish: el centro
English: center, bullseye
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Mi flecha quedó en el {{c1::centro}}.
Example (EN): My arrow landed in the center.
Notes: The ten ring is "el diez". In a city "el centro" means downtown.

id: seed-es-archery-021
Spanish: la puntuación
English: score
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): ¿Cuál fue tu {{c1::puntuación}}?
Example (EN): What was your score?
Notes: "El puntaje" is also common in Latin America. "Los puntos" = points.

id: seed-es-archery-022
Spanish: la ronda
English: round (of a competition)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Pasé a la segunda {{c1::ronda}}.
Example (EN): I advanced to the second round.
links: collocation [[seed-es-archery-023]]

id: seed-es-archery-023
Spanish: la competencia
English: competition, tournament
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Mañana tengo una {{c1::competencia}} de tiro con arco.
Example (EN): Tomorrow I have an archery competition.
Notes: Mexico: "competencia"; Spain: "competición". Also means "the competitors / rivals".

id: seed-es-archery-024
Spanish: el campo de tiro
English: shooting range
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::campo de tiro}} abre a las ocho.
Example (EN): The range opens at eight.
links: same root [[seed-es-archery-019]]

id: seed-es-archery-025
Spanish: el arco recurvo
English: recurve bow
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::arco recurvo}} es el que se usa en los Juegos Olímpicos.
Example (EN): The recurve bow is the one used in the Olympic Games.

id: seed-es-archery-026
Spanish: el arco compuesto
English: compound bow
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::arco compuesto}} tiene poleas.
Example (EN): The compound bow has cams.
Notes: "Las poleas" = cams / pulleys.

id: seed-es-archery-027
Spanish: el disparador
English: release aid
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Con el arco compuesto se usa un {{c1::disparador}}.
Example (EN): With a compound bow you use a release aid.
links: collocation [[seed-es-archery-026]]; same root [[seed-es-archery-035]]

id: seed-es-archery-028
Spanish: el reposaflechas
English: arrow rest
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): La flecha se apoya en el {{c1::reposaflechas}}.
Example (EN): The arrow sits on the arrow rest.
Notes: Same form in the plural: los reposaflechas. Also "el apoyaflechas".

id: seed-es-archery-029
Spanish: las palas
English: limbs (of a bow)
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Las {{c1::palas}} del arco se doblan al tensar.
Example (EN): The bow's limbs bend when you draw.
Notes: Also "las ramas" or "los brazos". Singular "la pala" also means shovel.

id: seed-es-archery-030
Spanish: la empuñadura
English: grip, handle
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): No aprietes la {{c1::empuñadura}}.
Example (EN): Don't squeeze the grip.
Notes: The middle section of the bow is "el cuerpo" or "el riser".

id: seed-es-archery-031
Spanish: tensar
English: to draw (a bow), to tighten
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): {{c1::Tensa}} el arco despacio.
Example (EN): Draw the bow slowly.
Notes: Also "abrir el arco".
links: same root [[seed-es-archery-032]]; collocation [[seed-es-archery-007]]

id: seed-es-archery-032
Spanish: tenso
English: tense, taut
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): Tus hombros están muy {{c1::tensos}}.
Example (EN): Your shoulders are very tense.

id: seed-es-archery-033
Spanish: apuntar
English: to aim
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): {{c1::Apunta}} al centro de la diana.
Example (EN): Aim at the center of the target.
Notes: Also means "to write down".

id: seed-es-archery-034
Spanish: soltar
English: to release, to let go
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): {{c1::Suelta}} la cuerda suavemente.
Example (EN): Release the string smoothly.
Notes: Stem-changing (o → ue): suelto, sueltas. Noun: "la suelta" = the release.

id: seed-es-archery-035
Spanish: disparar
English: to shoot
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Esperen el silbato antes de {{c1::disparar}}.
Example (EN): Wait for the whistle before shooting.
Notes: In archery "tirar" is also very common: "¿A qué distancia tiras?".

id: seed-es-archery-036
Spanish: anclar
English: to anchor
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): {{c1::Ancla}} la mano debajo de la barbilla.
Example (EN): Anchor your hand under your chin.
links: same root [[seed-es-archery-037]]

id: seed-es-archery-037
Spanish: el anclaje
English: anchor point
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Busca siempre el mismo {{c1::anclaje}}.
Example (EN): Always find the same anchor point.
Notes: Also "el punto de anclaje".

id: seed-es-archery-038
Spanish: la postura
English: stance, posture
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Una buena {{c1::postura}} mejora la precisión.
Example (EN): A good stance improves accuracy.

id: seed-es-archery-039
Spanish: recoger
English: to collect, to pick up
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Vamos a {{c1::recoger}} las flechas.
Example (EN): Let's go collect the arrows.
Notes: Spelling change: yo recojo. Also "ir por las flechas".

id: seed-es-archery-040
Spanish: fallar
English: to miss
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): {{c1::Fallé}} el último tiro.
Example (EN): I missed the last shot.
links: antonym [[seed-es-archery-041]]

id: seed-es-archery-041
Spanish: acertar
English: to hit (the target), to get right
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Hoy {{c1::acerté}} casi todas mis flechas.
Example (EN): Today almost all my arrows hit the target.
Notes: Stem-changing (e → ie): acierto, aciertas.

id: seed-es-archery-042
Spanish: el libraje
English: draw weight
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Empieza con un {{c1::libraje}} bajo.
Example (EN): Start with a low draw weight.
Notes: Measured in pounds ("libras"). Also "la potencia del arco".

id: seed-es-archery-043
Spanish: ¡Alto!
English: Stop!, Hold!
Gender / POS: interjection
Example (ES): ¡{{c1::Alto}}! Hay alguien en el campo.
Example (EN): Stop! There's someone on the range.
Notes: Safety call: everyone stops shooting immediately. Also the word on Mexican stop signs.

id: seed-es-archery-044
Spanish: ¡Cuidado!
English: Careful!, Watch out!
Gender / POS: interjection
Example (ES): ¡{{c1::Cuidado}}! No camines frente a la línea.
Example (EN): Watch out! Don't walk in front of the line.
Notes: Informal Mexican alternative: "¡Aguas!".

id: seed-es-archery-045
Spanish: la seguridad
English: safety, security
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::seguridad}} es lo más importante en el campo de tiro.
Example (EN): Safety is the most important thing at the range.

id: seed-es-archery-046
Spanish: el silbato
English: whistle
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Tres toques de {{c1::silbato}}: pueden ir a recoger las flechas.
Example (EN): Three whistle blasts: you may go collect your arrows.
Notes: Usual range signals: 2 = go to the line, 1 = start shooting, 3 = collect arrows, 4 or more = stop immediately.

id: seed-es-archery-047
Spanish: la tanda
English: end (a set of arrows shot)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Cada {{c1::tanda}} es de seis flechas.
Example (EN): Each end is six arrows.
Notes: Some ranges say "la serie".

id: seed-es-archery-048
Spanish: el juez
English: judge, referee
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::juez}} revisa las flechas dudosas.
Example (EN): The judge checks the borderline arrows.
Notes: Plural: los jueces. Female: la jueza (or la juez).

id: seed-es-archery-049
Spanish: el viento
English: wind
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Hoy hay mucho {{c1::viento}}; apunta un poco a la izquierda.
Example (EN): It's very windy today; aim a little to the left.

id: seed-es-archery-050
Spanish: el ojo dominante
English: dominant eye
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Mi {{c1::ojo dominante}} es el derecho.
Example (EN): My dominant eye is the right one.

# ===== Topic 3: Gym & weightlifting =====
@deck Gimnasio / Pesas
@type Spanish Vocab
@tags gym
@languages es-MX en-US

id: seed-es-gym-001
Spanish: el gimnasio
English: gym
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Voy al {{c1::gimnasio}} cuatro veces por semana.
Example (EN): I go to the gym four times a week.
Notes: Colloquial in Mexico: "el gym" (pronounced "yim").

id: seed-es-gym-002
Spanish: las pesas
English: weights
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Me gusta levantar {{c1::pesas}}.
Example (EN): I like lifting weights.
Notes: "Hacer pesas" = to lift weights. Singular: la pesa.
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-024]]

id: seed-es-gym-003
Spanish: la barra
English: barbell, bar
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Pon más discos en la {{c1::barra}}.
Example (EN): Put more plates on the bar.
Notes: "La barra olímpica" = Olympic barbell. "La barra fija" = pull-up bar.
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-005]]

id: seed-es-gym-004
Spanish: las mancuernas
English: dumbbells
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Usa {{c1::mancuernas}} de diez kilos.
Example (EN): Use ten-kilo dumbbells.
Notes: Singular: la mancuerna. Also simply called "pesas".

id: seed-es-gym-005
Spanish: los discos
English: plates (weight plates)
Gender / POS: m. pl. noun
Example (ES): Guarda los {{c1::discos}} cuando termines.
Example (EN): Put the plates away when you're done.
Notes: Singular: el disco.

id: seed-es-gym-006
Spanish: el banco
English: bench
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Ajusta el {{c1::banco}} a 30 grados.
Example (EN): Set the bench to 30 degrees.
Notes: Also means "bank".

id: seed-es-gym-007
Spanish: el rack
English: squat rack, power rack
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¿Me prestas el {{c1::rack}} cuando termines?
Example (EN): Can I use the rack when you're done?
Notes: English loanword used in Mexican gyms. A power cage is also "la jaula".

id: seed-es-gym-008
Spanish: las rodilleras
English: knee sleeves, knee pads
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Me pongo las {{c1::rodilleras}} para hacer sentadillas.
Example (EN): I put on my knee sleeves to do squats.
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-043]]

id: seed-es-gym-009
Spanish: el cinturón
English: lifting belt, belt
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Usa el {{c1::cinturón}} en las series pesadas.
Example (EN): Use the belt on heavy sets.
Notes: Plural: los cinturones. In Mexico a support belt is also "la faja".

id: seed-es-gym-010
Spanish: la máquina
English: machine
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Esta {{c1::máquina}} es para las piernas.
Example (EN): This machine is for the legs.

id: seed-es-gym-011
Spanish: la sentadilla
English: squat
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Hoy hago cinco series de {{c1::sentadillas}}.
Example (EN): Today I'm doing five sets of squats.
Notes: "Sentadilla frontal" = front squat; "sentadilla búlgara" = Bulgarian split squat.
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-008]]

id: seed-es-gym-012
Spanish: el peso muerto
English: deadlift
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::peso muerto}} trabaja toda la espalda.
Example (EN): The deadlift works your whole back.
Notes: Literally "dead weight". "Peso muerto rumano" = Romanian deadlift.
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-003]]

id: seed-es-gym-013
Spanish: el press de banca
English: bench press
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Mi récord en {{c1::press de banca}} es de 80 kilos.
Example (EN): My bench press record is 80 kilos.
Notes: Also "press de pecho" or "press plano".
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-006]]

id: seed-es-gym-014
Spanish: el press militar
English: overhead press, military press
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::press militar}} fortalece los hombros.
Example (EN): The overhead press strengthens the shoulders.
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-040]]

id: seed-es-gym-015
Spanish: las dominadas
English: pull-ups, chin-ups
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Todavía no puedo hacer {{c1::dominadas}}.
Example (EN): I still can't do pull-ups.
Notes: Related Mexican gym term: "jalón al pecho" = lat pulldown (from "jalar", to pull; Spain: "tirar").

id: seed-es-gym-016
Spanish: el remo
English: row (exercise)
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Haz el {{c1::remo}} con la espalda recta.
Example (EN): Do the row with a straight back.
Notes: Also means "oar" or "rowing". "Remo con barra" = barbell row.

id: seed-es-gym-017
Spanish: las zancadas
English: lunges
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Hice tres series de {{c1::zancadas}}.
Example (EN): I did three sets of lunges.
Notes: In Mexico also "los desplantes".

id: seed-es-gym-018
Spanish: los abdominales
English: abs, ab exercises, crunches
Gender / POS: m. pl. noun
Example (ES): Termino cada rutina con {{c1::abdominales}}.
Example (EN): I finish every workout with ab exercises.
Notes: Refers to both the muscles and the exercises.
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-046]]

id: seed-es-gym-019
Spanish: las lagartijas
English: push-ups
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Haz veinte {{c1::lagartijas}}.
Example (EN): Do twenty push-ups.
Notes: Mexican usage (literally "little lizards"). Spain: "las flexiones".

id: seed-es-gym-020
Spanish: la serie
English: set
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Me falta una {{c1::serie}} más.
Example (EN): I have one more set left.
Notes: Also means "TV series".
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-021]]

id: seed-es-gym-021
Spanish: la repetición
English: rep, repetition
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Haz diez {{c1::repeticiones}} por serie.
Example (EN): Do ten reps per set.
Notes: Colloquial: "las reps".

id: seed-es-gym-022
Spanish: el descanso
English: rest, break
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Toma un {{c1::descanso}} de dos minutos entre series.
Example (EN): Take a two-minute rest between sets.
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-023]]

id: seed-es-gym-023
Spanish: descansar
English: to rest
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Los músculos crecen cuando {{c1::descansas}}.
Example (EN): Your muscles grow when you rest.

id: seed-es-gym-024
Spanish: el peso
English: weight
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¿Cuánto {{c1::peso}} le pusiste a la barra?
Example (EN): How much weight did you put on the bar?
Notes: Also the Mexican currency (el peso mexicano).
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-025]]

id: seed-es-gym-025
Spanish: pesado
English: heavy
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): Esta barra está muy {{c1::pesada}}.
Example (EN): This bar is very heavy.
Notes: Said of a person, it means "annoying": "¡Qué pesado!".
links: antonym [[seed-es-gym-026]]

id: seed-es-gym-026
Spanish: ligero
English: light (in weight)
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): Empieza con un peso {{c1::ligero}}.
Example (EN): Start with a light weight.
Notes: Also "liviano".

id: seed-es-gym-027
Spanish: levantar
English: to lift, to raise
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): {{c1::Levanto}} pesas los lunes, miércoles y viernes.
Example (EN): I lift weights on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays.
Notes: "Levantarse" = to get up.
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-002]]

id: seed-es-gym-028
Spanish: entrenar
English: to train, to work out
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): ¿A qué hora {{c1::entrenas}}?
Example (EN): What time do you work out?
Notes: Also "hacer ejercicio".
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-029]]

id: seed-es-gym-029
Spanish: el entrenador
English: trainer, coach (male)
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Mi {{c1::entrenador}} me corrige la técnica.
Example (EN): My trainer corrects my form.
Notes: "Entrenador personal" = personal trainer. Also "el coach".
links: gender pair [[seed-es-gym-030]]

id: seed-es-gym-030
Spanish: la entrenadora
English: trainer, coach (female)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::entrenadora}} nos dio una rutina nueva.
Example (EN): The trainer gave us a new routine.

id: seed-es-gym-031
Spanish: calentar
English: to warm up
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Siempre {{c1::caliento}} antes de levantar.
Example (EN): I always warm up before lifting.
Notes: Stem-changing (e → ie): caliento, calientas.
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-032]]

id: seed-es-gym-032
Spanish: el calentamiento
English: warm-up
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::calentamiento}} dura diez minutos.
Example (EN): The warm-up lasts ten minutes.

id: seed-es-gym-033
Spanish: estirar
English: to stretch
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Hay que {{c1::estirar}} después de entrenar.
Example (EN): You should stretch after working out.
Notes: Noun: "el estiramiento" (stretch).

id: seed-es-gym-034
Spanish: la fuerza
English: strength, force
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Quiero ganar {{c1::fuerza}}.
Example (EN): I want to get stronger.
links: same root [[seed-es-gym-035]]

id: seed-es-gym-035
Spanish: fuerte
English: strong
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): Estás muy {{c1::fuerte}}.
Example (EN): You're very strong.
Notes: Also "loud". Informal Mexican slang for very muscular: "mamado".

id: seed-es-gym-036
Spanish: el músculo
English: muscle
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Me duele este {{c1::músculo}}.
Example (EN): This muscle hurts.
Notes: "Ganar músculo" = to build muscle.

id: seed-es-gym-037
Spanish: la espalda
English: back
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Hoy toca {{c1::espalda}} y bíceps.
Example (EN): Today is back and biceps.
Notes: "Hoy toca…" = today it's time for…

id: seed-es-gym-038
Spanish: el pecho
English: chest
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El press de banca trabaja el {{c1::pecho}}.
Example (EN): The bench press works the chest.

id: seed-es-gym-039
Spanish: la pierna
English: leg
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Nunca te saltes el día de {{c1::pierna}}.
Example (EN): Never skip leg day.
Notes: Gym talk uses the singular: "día de pierna".

id: seed-es-gym-040
Spanish: el hombro
English: shoulder
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Me duele el {{c1::hombro}} derecho.
Example (EN): My right shoulder hurts.

id: seed-es-gym-041
Spanish: el brazo
English: arm
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Estira bien los {{c1::brazos}}.
Example (EN): Fully extend your arms.

id: seed-es-gym-042
Spanish: los glúteos
English: glutes
Gender / POS: m. pl. noun
Example (ES): Las sentadillas trabajan los {{c1::glúteos}}.
Example (EN): Squats work the glutes.
Notes: Singular: el glúteo.

id: seed-es-gym-043
Spanish: la rodilla
English: knee
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): No dejes que la {{c1::rodilla}} se vaya hacia adentro.
Example (EN): Don't let your knee cave in.

id: seed-es-gym-044
Spanish: la cadera
English: hip, hips
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Empuja la {{c1::cadera}} hacia atrás.
Example (EN): Push your hips back.

id: seed-es-gym-045
Spanish: ¡Espalda recta!
English: Keep your back straight!
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): ¡{{c1::Espalda recta}}! No te encorves.
Example (EN): Keep your back straight! Don't hunch over.
links: collocation [[seed-es-gym-037]]

id: seed-es-gym-046
Spanish: ¡Aprieta el abdomen!
English: Brace your core!, Tighten your abs!
Gender / POS: phrase
Example (ES): Antes de bajar, ¡{{c1::aprieta el abdomen}}!
Example (EN): Before you go down, brace your core!
Notes: "Apretar" is stem-changing (e → ie); "aprieta" is the tú command. Also "¡Activa el core!".

id: seed-es-gym-047
Spanish: el agarre
English: grip
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Usa un {{c1::agarre}} más abierto.
Example (EN): Use a wider grip.
Notes: Verb: "agarrar" (to grab, to take), the everyday verb in Mexico. Avoid Spain's "coger": it is vulgar in Mexico.

id: seed-es-gym-048
Spanish: adolorido
English: sore, aching
Gender / POS: adj.
Example (ES): Estoy {{c1::adolorido}} por el entrenamiento de ayer.
Example (EN): I'm sore from yesterday's workout.
Notes: Spain: "dolorido", or "tener agujetas" for muscle soreness.

id: seed-es-gym-049
Spanish: la proteína
English: protein
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Me tomo un licuado de {{c1::proteína}} después de entrenar.
Example (EN): I have a protein shake after working out.
Notes: Mexico: "el licuado" = shake or smoothie (Spain: "el batido").

id: seed-es-gym-050
Spanish: sudar
English: to sweat
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): {{c1::Sudé}} muchísimo en la clase.
Example (EN): I sweated a ton in class.
Notes: Noun: "el sudor".

# ===== Topic 4: Cats =====
@deck Gatos
@type Spanish Vocab
@tags cats
@languages es-MX en-US

id: seed-es-cats-001
Spanish: el gato
English: cat, male cat
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Mi {{c1::gato}} duerme todo el día.
Example (EN): My cat sleeps all day.
Notes: In Mexico "el gato" is also tic-tac-toe, the # sign, and a car jack.
links: gender pair [[seed-es-cats-002]]; same root [[seed-es-cats-003]]; synonym [[seed-es-cats-005]]

id: seed-es-cats-002
Spanish: la gata
English: cat (female)
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::gata}} tuvo cuatro gatitos.
Example (EN): The cat had four kittens.
Notes: Careful: in Mexico "gata" is also a derogatory slang word for a maid; with cats it's perfectly normal.

id: seed-es-cats-003
Spanish: el gatito
English: kitten, kitty
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): El {{c1::gatito}} juega con una pelota.
Example (EN): The kitten is playing with a ball.
Notes: Diminutive of "gato" (-ito).
links: gender pair [[seed-es-cats-004]]

id: seed-es-cats-004
Spanish: la gatita
English: kitten (female), kitty
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): Adoptamos una {{c1::gatita}} blanca.
Example (EN): We adopted a white kitten.

id: seed-es-cats-005
Spanish: el michi
English: kitty, cat (colloquial)
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): ¡Mira ese {{c1::michi}} tan bonito!
Example (EN): Look at that cute kitty!
Notes: Very common in Mexico and online; also "michito". To call a cat people say "mish, mish" or "michi, michi".

id: seed-es-cats-006
Spanish: los bigotes
English: whiskers
Gender / POS: m. pl. noun
Example (ES): Nunca le cortes los {{c1::bigotes}} a un gato.
Example (EN): Never cut a cat's whiskers.
Notes: Singular "el bigote" also means mustache.

id: seed-es-cats-007
Spanish: la cola
English: tail
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): El gato mueve la {{c1::cola}} cuando está molesto.
Example (EN): The cat swishes its tail when it's annoyed.
Notes: In Spain "la cola" is also a line of people; Mexico usually says "la fila".

id: seed-es-cats-008
Spanish: las garras
English: claws
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): El gato sacó las {{c1::garras}}.
Example (EN): The cat put its claws out.
Notes: "Las uñas" (nails) is also common: "cortarle las uñas al gato".

id: seed-es-cats-009
Spanish: las almohadillas
English: paw pads, toe beans
Gender / POS: f. pl. noun
Example (ES): Mi gato tiene las {{c1::almohadillas}} rosadas.
Example (EN): My cat has pink paw pads.
Notes: "La almohada" = pillow. "Las patitas" = little paws.

id: seed-es-cats-010
Spanish: el pelaje
English: fur, coat
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Tu gata tiene un {{c1::pelaje}} muy suave.
Example (EN): Your cat has very soft fur.
Notes: Everyday word: "el pelo".
links: same root [[seed-es-cats-046]]

id: seed-es-cats-011
Spanish: la oreja
English: ear
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): El gato echó las {{c1::orejas}} hacia atrás.
Example (EN): The cat flattened its ears back.
Notes: "La oreja" = outer ear; "el oído" = inner ear, hearing.

id: seed-es-cats-012
Spanish: la lengua
English: tongue
Gender / POS: f. noun
Example (ES): La {{c1::lengua}} del gato es muy áspera.
Example (EN): A cat's tongue is very rough.
Notes: Also means "language".

id: seed-es-cats-013
Spanish: ronronear
English: to purr
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Mi gata {{c1::ronronea}} cuando la acaricio.
Example (EN): My cat purrs when I pet her.
links: same root [[seed-es-cats-014]]

id: seed-es-cats-014
Spanish: el ronroneo
English: purr, purring
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Me encanta el {{c1::ronroneo}} de mi gato.
Example (EN): I love my cat's purring.

id: seed-es-cats-015
Spanish: maullar
English: to meow
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): El gato {{c1::maúlla}} en la puerta.
Example (EN): The cat is meowing at the door.
Notes: Written accent on the stressed u: maúllo, maúllas, maúlla.
links: same root [[seed-es-cats-016]]

id: seed-es-cats-016
Spanish: el maullido
English: meow
Gender / POS: m. noun
Example (ES): Escuché un {{c1::maullido}} afuera.
Example (EN): I heard a meow outside.
Notes: The sound itself is written "miau".

id: seed-es-cats-017
Spanish: arañar
English: to scratch (with claws)
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): El gato {{c1::arañó}} el sillón.
Example (EN): The cat scratched the armchair.
Notes: Noun: "el arañazo".
links: synonym [[seed-es-cats-018]]; collocation [[seed-es-cats-032]]

id: seed-es-cats-018
Spanish: rasguñar
English: to scratch
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Cuidado, mi gato {{c1::rasguña}}.
Example (EN): Careful, my cat scratches.
Notes: Very common in Mexico; noun: "el rasguño". For scratching an itch use "rascarse".

id: seed-es-cats-019
Spanish: bufar
English: to hiss
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): La gata {{c1::bufó}} cuando vio al perro.
Example (EN): The cat hissed when she saw the dog.
Notes: Noun: "el bufido".

id: seed-es-cats-020
Spanish: acicalarse
English: to groom oneself
Gender / POS: reflexive verb
Example (ES): Los gatos {{c1::se acicalan}} varias horas al día.
Example (EN): Cats groom themselves several hours a day.
Notes: Also "lamerse" or "asearse".

id: seed-es-cats-021
Spanish: lamer
English: to lick
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Mi gato me {{c1::lame}} la mano.
Example (EN): My cat licks my hand.
links: collocation [[seed-es-cats-012]]

id: seed-es-cats-022
Spanish: dormir
English: to sleep
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): Los gatos {{c1::duermen}} hasta 16 horas al día.
Example (EN): Cats sleep up to 16 hours a day.
Notes: Stem-changing (o → ue). "Tomar una siesta" = to take a nap.

id: seed-es-cats-023
Spanish: saltar
English: to jump
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): La gatita {{c1::saltó}} a la mesa.
Example (EN): The kitten jumped onto the table.

id: seed-es-cats-024
Spanish: cazar
English: to hunt
Gender / POS: verb
Example (ES): A mi gato le gusta {{c1::cazar}} moscas.
Example (EN): My cat likes to hunt flies.
Notes: Spelling change: yo cacé.
links: collocation [[seed-es-cats-049]]

id: seed-es-cats-025
Spanish: esconderse
English: to hide
Gender / POS: reflexive verb
Example (ES): El gato {{c1::se esconde}} debajo de la cama.
Example (EN): The cat hides under the bed.

id: seed-es-cats-026
Spanish: estirarse
English: to stretch (oneself)
Gender / POS: reflexive verb
Example (ES): Mi gata {{c1::se estira}} al despertar.
Example (EN): My cat stretches when she wakes up.
